WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:12.560
Big data can come from several different&nbsp;
sources. One way to think about it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:16.320
whether the data was produced by a human&nbsp;
or whether it was produced by a machine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:22.000
Human generate a lot of data whether we&nbsp;
mean to or not. The first thing that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.040
want to talk about is intentional data. This&nbsp;
is the data that you know you are creating.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:33.280
For instance, if you take photos, videos, or&nbsp;
record audio or put text on a social network,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:37.360
you know you’re doing it. You can also&nbsp;
click “like” if you’re on Facebook.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:42.480
When you do web searches, a record of the web&nbsp;
pages that you have viewed are bookmarked.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:50.400
Your emails and your text messages. Your cell&nbsp;
phone calls. If you read an eBook, the highlights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:55.840
the notes in the bookmarks and online purchases.&nbsp;
All of these are kinds of data that do not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:00.640
exist until the person deliberately makes them&nbsp;
happen, so these are records of human actions.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:08.480
What is interesting is that, in addition to&nbsp;
these intentional pieces of information, there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:16.080
also meta data. Meta data is data about data. You&nbsp;
might call this second order human generated data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:22.240
The idea is that, this is data that accompanies&nbsp;
the things that you do, and you may not be aware&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:28.560
of it. Interestingly, meta data can be enormous,&nbsp;
sometimes larger than the actual piece of data you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:34.640
created. Perhaps, meta data is more significant&nbsp;
than the actual data for the big data world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:38.720
because it is computer generated and is&nbsp;
already machine readable and searchable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:42.960
Here are a few examples of meta&nbsp;
data and what can be done with it.&nbsp;

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.880
For instance, if you take a picture with&nbsp;
your phone, you not only get the picture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:57.120
you also get what’s called the EXIF data. That&nbsp;
stands for exchangeable image file format,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:00.160
and this is the meta data that&nbsp;
comes from a picture on an iPhone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:06.240
Aside from the name of the file which you see&nbsp;
at the top left and that it’s 3.1 megabytes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:11.600
and the time that it was taken, near the bottom&nbsp;
on the right side, you can see the GPS altitude,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:57.840
latitude, longitude and position, or partway&nbsp;
up from that, you’ll even see the GPS image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:04.160
direction. It knows which way you’re holding the&nbsp;
camera. This is an enormous amount of information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:09.205
that accompanies the photo that you get.
Another interesting thing is cell phone meta data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.205 --> 00:03:09.298
There is a lot of information that accompanies&nbsp;
your phone call. What is interesting is that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.298 --> 00:03:09.388
with just four calls where you know the time&nbsp;
and the position, in an anonymized data set,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.388 --> 00:03:09.460
that is enough information to identify&nbsp;
95% of individuals. Fortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:09.555
this information is not publicly available. But&nbsp;
the point here is that, it is possible to tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.555 --> 00:03:11.360
a lot about people by the meta data.
Another example is email meta data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:15.040
There is a lot of information&nbsp;
that accompanies each email.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:19.840
The four very common pieces are:&nbsp;
who is it from, who is it going to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:25.840
did they CC anybody, carbon copy anybody, and&nbsp;
the time that it was sent. Now, one really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:30.960
interesting thing about this that you can do for&nbsp;
yourself is that MIT has created a web app called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:35.760
Immersion that allows you to do a quick analysis&nbsp;
of your own social network via your email account.&nbsp;

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:45.040
I am going to show you the Immersion&nbsp;
website. It’s immersion.media.mit.edu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:50.000
and what it does is, it takes those four pieces&nbsp;
of information about your emails and it puts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:55.760
together an image of the network. For example,&nbsp;
here is the Immersion demo with fictional data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:02.480
Imagine a fictionalized character, Tony&nbsp;
Stark. He has information six years of emails,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:09.760
20,000 emails to 159 collaborators, and what you&nbsp;
can see is who is in his group. We have more to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:15.600
Travis than anybody else. It looks like Travis&nbsp;
is connected with Tabitha, and you can change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:20.960
for instance, how things are distributed over&nbsp;
here. The number of notes that show, now we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:26.240
have more and more people showing up. We can make&nbsp;
that a little bit smaller, and the strength of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:31.920
links between them. What is interesting is that,&nbsp;
for instance, when you do it to your mailbox, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:36.480
might be able to find distinct group that shows&nbsp;
for your family, for my job, and for people that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:42.080
you socialize with. There are sometimes people&nbsp;
who connect several of those groups at once, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:47.600
the interesting is that, this is just based on who&nbsp;
you sent the email to, who it was from, the CCs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:52.240
the time and the date, and that is how it is able&nbsp;
to reconstruct your own personal social network.&nbsp;

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:57.760
The last thing I want to&nbsp;
show you is about Twitter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:02.880
Now, one of the interesting things about&nbsp;
Twitter is that, tweets are very small.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:09.600
They are limited to 140 characters. What is&nbsp;
interesting about this from a research point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:13.760
of view, is there is an enormous amount&nbsp;
of meta data that accompanies each tweet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:20.560
This image here was put together by an employee at&nbsp;
Twitter, Raffi Krikorian. The information in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:26.880
red is the content of the tweet. That is the stuff&nbsp;
that you meant to put there. But, look at all this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:31.520
other information that accompanies it in terms of&nbsp;
whether there was a reply, a truncated version,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:36.720
the author’s screen name and URL, the location,&nbsp;
the creation data, the account, the number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:43.040
followers, the time zone, their language, whether&nbsp;
they have a verified badge, the place ID, the URL,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:49.280
the country, the bounding box, the application&nbsp;
they sent it, and etc. It is an enormous amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:54.960
of information. The meta data in this case is&nbsp;
several times greater than the actual content.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:00.320
This is one of the most interesting things,&nbsp;
and this is why Twitter, in particular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:05.840
is a very rich data set for people who are doing&nbsp;
marketing research or social connection research.&nbsp;

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:12.960
In summary, there are all sources of big data,&nbsp;
and one of the interesting things about it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:18.960
that the meta data in particular, does not have&nbsp;
to be processed. It is already computer readable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:24.480
It is searchable. It is mineable, and&nbsp;
you can start to get information about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:33.760
it immediately to reach your&nbsp;
big data analysis purposes.

